.TH PMASK 1 "2026" "pmask 0.1.0-beta.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pmask \- run a process tree with a redacted /proc entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.br
.B pmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-args\-from\-fd\fR \fIN\fR
.br
.B pmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-image\fR \fIPATH\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pmask
is a ptrace supervisor.
It runs a program and every process that program spawns.
Each process shows a presentation label in
.BR /proc/ pid /cmdline
and
.BR /proc/ pid /comm
instead of its real command line and process name.
Every other local account sees the label.
The program itself receives its real arguments and environment.
.PP
A process is syscall-supervised until its first exec completes.
At the execve entry stop the supervisor rewrites the argument and
environment pointer arrays of the old image.
The kernel then copies the label and empty strings, so the real command
line never exists in the new image.
At the exec completion stop the supervisor writes the real strings into
an anonymous mapping injected inside the tracee and repoints the
arrays.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-label " \fINAME\fR"
Presentation label.
The value
.B \-
gives a blank label (one space).
The label must not contain NUL, LF or CR.
Default: the program name, with all arguments removed.
.TP
.B \-\-label\-empty
Empty label.
.TP
.BI \-\-argv0 " NAME"
argv[0] of the program.
Default: the command exactly as typed.
.TP
.BI \-\-comm " NAME"
Process name for
.BR top ,
at most 15 bytes.
Default: the label cut to 15 bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-env
Leave
.B /proc/\fIpid\fB/environ
visible.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep\-env= NAME[,NAME...]
Keep only the named variables visible.
The inline form is required.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-env " K=V"
Add or replace one variable.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " PATH"
Read variables from a file: one KEY=VALUE per line, # starts a comment
line, blank lines are skipped, values are taken as written.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-args\-from\-fd " N"
Read the command from file descriptor N as a NUL-separated vector:
program first, then arguments.
The vector replaces
.IR COMMAND ,
so no command may follow the options.
The descriptor is duplicated before it is read, so the caller's own
descriptor stays open; the argument source itself is consumed.
It is capped at 8 MiB; a larger vector is refused without buffering it
all.
.TP
.BI \-\-on\-exit " POLICY"
What happens to descendants still running when the command exits:
.B detach
(default; each settled descendant is detached and keeps the label),
.B wait
(supervise until the whole tree exits) or
.B kill
(send SIGKILL).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-follow
Redact only the direct child.
Descendants are traced but not redacted.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-exit\-kill
Do not set PTRACE_O_EXITKILL: the tree survives if the supervisor dies.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dumpable
Set PR_SET_DUMPABLE=0 in every process of the tree.
Same-uid observers then cannot read environ, maps, exe or fd.
Core dumps and debugger attach stop working.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-new\-privs
Set no_new_privs before exec.
Set-uid and file-capability programs gain nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-exact
Keep the original cmdline byte extent: the label is written over the
real arguments, but
.BR /proc/\fIpid\fB/stat
still reports how many bytes they took.
By default the kernel's argv and environment windows are moved onto the
label with
.BR prctl(PR_SET_MM_MAP) ,
so cmdline is exactly the label and environ is empty (or keeps the
variables named by
.BR \-\-keep\-env ).
.TP
.B \-\-detach
Detach each process once its exec has settled, so
.B TracerPid
reads 0 for the payload.
This is a coverage trade: a detached process and everything it forks
later run unredacted, and the session summary counts each detach.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-rename
Revert a later prctl(PR_SET_NAME) to the label.
Installs a seccomp filter that traps that call only.
Implies
.BR \-\-no\-new\-privs .
If the filter cannot be installed, the run exits 126 naming the failure
instead of running without the promise.
.PP
With
.B \-\-no\-dumpable
the tracer may not be able to reach the label address inside a
non-dumpable tracee.
When that happens the rename is refused: the
.BR prctl(PR_SET_NAME)
call fails, the process keeps the label as its
.BR comm ,
and the run prints one warning and counts each refused rename in the
session summary.
A root tracer, whose access does not depend on dumpability, still
redirects the rename.
.TP
.B \-\-rexec
Exec the program from a sealed memfd, so
.B /proc/\fIpid\fB/exe
names the label.
The child tries the carriers in order:
.BR execveat ( AT_EMPTY_PATH ),
then
.BR /proc/self/fd/ N
with an ordinary
.BR execve ,
then a temporary file on a mount that allows execution.
Every failed step is reported and counted.
Without
.B \-\-rexec\-allow\-disk
a refused image ends the child with 126 (127 when the image is
missing) instead of running the on-disk file.
.TP
.B \-\-rexec\-allow\-disk
Add the on-disk path as the explicit last resort of the
.B \-\-rexec
chain.
The fallback is reported and counted; it is never automatic.
.TP
.BI \-\-image " PATH"
Read the program bytes from PATH and exec them from a sealed image.
PATH is opened as given, not searched in PATH.
.B \-\-image \-
reads standard input to end of file.
Only ELF images work; a script needs an interpreter that opens a path.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.BI \-\-image\-from\-fd " N"
Read the program bytes from descriptor N.
The payload's
.B argv[0]
is the placeholder
.B "<fd image>"
unless
.B \-\-argv0
gives one, because a descriptor has no path to name.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.B \-\-exec\-once
Reuse one sealed image per distinct program, keyed by the resolved path
and its mtime, across the execs of the session.
Later execs use a guarded temporary-file image, because the supervisor
is non-dumpable and the tracee cannot read its descriptors.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.B \-\-hugetlb
Ask for a hugetlbfs-backed image and report the backing it got.
When huge pages are unavailable the run reports it and uses an ordinary
memfd.
The fallback covers every image source, including
.B \-\-image \-
and
.BR \-\-image\-from\-fd :
a pipe source is spooled first, so a failed huge-page write is retried
on an ordinary memfd instead of ending the run.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.B \-\-unshare
Run in new user, pid and mount namespaces with a private /proc, when
the kernel allows it.
Prints one line and continues without it otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope\-name " NAME"
Re-exec once through
.BR "systemd-run \-\-user \-\-scope" .
The transient unit is named
.BI pmask- NAME \- pid\fR,
with NAME sanitised to letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen and
cut to 64 bytes; its description is
.BR pmask ,
not the command line.
The unit name is visible in
.BR /proc/\fIpid\fB/cgroup
and in
.BR systemctl .
When
.B systemd-run
is not usable the run prints one line and continues without a scope.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress diagnostics, including the session summary and the isolation
report.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Trace every redaction event to stderr.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help text.
.SH MODES
The run selects one of three modes before any secret is read.
.TP
.B redact
The default where ptrace is permitted.
The tracer redacts every process's
.BR cmdline ,
.B environ
and
.B comm
and applies the mask options.
.TP
.B isolate
The payload runs in fresh namespaces without a tracer.
Selected by
.B \-\-isolate
when unprivileged user namespaces are available, and by
.B \-\-no\-redact \-\-isolate
when the host grants them.
No /proc metadata is redacted.
.TP
.B plain
No tracer and no namespace.
Selected by
.BR \-\-no\-redact ,
by a host that refuses ptrace, and by
.B \-\-isolate
where user namespaces are unavailable and ptrace is unavailable.
.PP
Selection order:
.B \-\-redact
and
.B \-\-no\-redact
together are a usage error.
.B \-\-redact \-\-isolate=LIST
redacts the tree and creates the namespaces LIST names.
.B \-\-isolate=none
creates no namespace and the ordinary ladder decides between redact and
plain.
.B \-\-no\-redact \-\-isolate
still creates the namespaces when the host grants them, because
isolation does not need ptrace.
Without either flag the run redacts where ptrace works and runs plain
where it does not; the probe's reason is printed once.
.PP
Options that need the tracer do not apply in isolate or plain mode.
They are
.BR \-\-comm ,
.BR \-\-argv0 ,
.BR \-\-no\-exact ,
.BR \-\-detach ,
.BR \-\-no\-follow ,
.BR \-\-on\-exit ,
.BR \-\-watch\-rename ,
.BR \-\-unshare ,
.BR \-\-no\-dumpable
and the image family
.RB ( \-\-rexec ,
.BR \-\-rexec\-allow\-disk ,
.BR \-\-image ,
.BR \-\-image\-from\-fd ,
.BR \-\-exec\-once ,
.B \-\-hugetlb ).
The run names each one it drops in a note, and the JSON report carries
the same notes.
Three flags are refused instead, because a note would not keep their
contract:
.B \-\-audit
needs a tracer,
.B \-\-image
(and the rest of the image family) cannot exec an image without the
redaction child, and
.B \-\-secret\-close\-on\-exec
needs a tracer once a secret is delivered.
.PP
The label and
.B \-\-label\-empty
are a /proc presentation, so they do not apply outside redaction;
.B \-\-keep\-env
is satisfied because the other modes do not redact
.BR environ ;
.B \-\-no\-new\-privs
is satisfied in isolate mode, which sets it.
.BR \-\-env ,
.BR \-\-env\-file ,
.B \-\-secret
and
.B \-\-scope\-name
apply in every mode.
.PP
In a mode that does not apply
.BR \-\-no\-dumpable ,
the report's
.B payload_dumpable
describes the selected mode: it is true unless the mode itself made
the payload non-dumpable.
.SH SECRET SIDE
These options and the subcommands below work even where ptrace is
refused, except
.BR \-\-secret\-close\-on\-exec ,
which needs the tracer and is refused in plain and isolate modes.
.TP
.BI \-\-secret " NAME=SRC"
Deliver a secret on descriptor 3, 4, ...; the child's environment
variable
.B PMASK_SECRET_<NAME>
holds the descriptor number, where NAME is the secret name upper-cased
with every byte that is not an ASCII letter or digit turned into an
underscore.
Two names that differ only in punctuation therefore collide, and the
run refuses them before any value is loaded.
SRC is one of
.BR file: PATH ,
.BR env: VAR ,
.BR agent: NAME ,
.B stdin
or
.BR literal: VAL .
Repeatable.
A
.B literal:
or
.B env:
source still sits in pmask's own command line or environment, where
/proc shows it; the value is absent from the child's /proc entry.
A
.B literal:
value is also in pmask's own argument vector, and the kernel audit
subsystem records the supervisor's own exec before pmask can redact
anything: the
.B type=EXECVE
and
.B type=PROCTITLE
records hold the value in clear text for the life of the audit log, and
the shell history and any wrapper's argv hold it too.
.B \-\-args\-from\-fd
keeps the value out of the supervisor's argv; the
.BR file: ,
.B agent:
and
.B stdin
sources never put it there.
.PP
The delivered descriptor survives exec, so every descendant inherits it
and a same-uid peer can read /proc/<pid>/fd/N, unless
.B \-\-secret\-close\-on\-exec
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Secret-agent socket for
.B agent: NAME
sources.
.TP
.BI \-\-isolate "[=LIST]"
Run the payload in fresh namespaces:
.B pid,net,tmp
|
.B all
|
.B none
(default all).
.B none
means no isolation at all: no namespace is created, and the payload
keeps the host's namespaces,
.BR no_new_privs
state and filesystem policy.
A host that cannot create user namespaces runs the plain path and says
so.
.PP
The private
.B /dev
carries
.BR /dev/null ,
.BR /dev/zero ,
.BR /dev/full ,
.BR /dev/random ,
.BR /dev/urandom
and
.BR /dev/tty ,
plus
.B /dev/pts
and
.B /dev/ptmx
when the host has them, and a private
.BR /dev/shm .
A member the host cannot provide is reported and counted as a
degradation.
.TP
.B \-\-redact
Ask for /proc redaction explicitly (the default).
With
.B \-\-isolate
it composes: the tree is redacted and the payload gets exactly the
namespaces LIST names, in one run.
A LIST the host cannot grant is reported as a degradation, never
replaced by a different set.
.B \-\-redact \-\-isolate=none
redacts the tree and creates no namespace.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-redact
Never touch /proc metadata, even where ptrace works.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-argv\-secret
Keep a
.B literal:
or
.B env:
secret source in a mode that cannot redact /proc, and print the
warning that names the disclosure, instead of refusing the run.
.TP
.B \-\-secret\-burn
Seal the delivered descriptors without
.BR F_SEAL_SHRINK ,
so the reader can truncate a value to zero once it has used it, and
cannot grow or rewrite it afterwards.
.TP
.B \-\-secret\-close\-on\-exec
The tracer closes each delivered descriptor in each direct child of the
payload at that child's fork.
The payload keeps the descriptor and reads its value after its own
exec.
The guarantee is for an ordinary fork/exec tree: a process that shares
the payload's descriptor table
.RB ( clone(CLONE_FILES)
without
.BR CLONE_THREAD )
cannot be closed without removing the payload's copy, so the run
reports a degradation and the session counts it.
With
.B \-\-detach
the policy cannot be enforced below a detached tracee, and the run
prints one warning.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Print one residue row per tracee at each exec completion, then a residue
count naming the interfaces the tree still discloses.
A field that cannot be read prints
.BR "not reachable" ,
never a zero.
A run whose selected mode is not redaction refuses the flag with exit
status 255, including a host that cannot ptrace and would select plain
mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-report " json"
Print the run as one JSON object on stdout.
The report is written after the payload has exited, on the same
descriptor 1 the payload inherited.
A payload that writes to stdout therefore interleaves with the report
and the stream is not one JSON document; send the payload's stdout
elsewhere when a consumer has to parse the report.
.B text
and
.B plain
select the default human-readable output.
The report is produced by the secret-side runner, so it needs
.BR \-\-secret ,
.B \-\-isolate
or
.BR \-\-no\-redact .
A plain mask run and a
.B \-\-daemon
job refuse the flag with exit status 255.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B run " [OPTIONS] [--] CMD"
The same parser as the default mode; accepts every option above.
.TP
.B read " NAME"
Print a secret that
.B run
delivered to this process.
One read per descriptor: the descriptor offset is shared with every
inheritor.
.TP
.B store " <sub>"
The encrypted store:
.BR init ,
.BR set\ NAME ,
.BR get\ NAME ,
.BR list ,
.BR rm\ NAME ,
.BR path .
Options:
.BI \-\-store " PATH"
(default $XDG_DATA_HOME/pmask/store.enc),
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " P"
and, for
.BR "set" ,
.BI \-\-from\-file " P"
(the value then comes from P instead of stdin).
.TP
.B agent " [OPTIONS]"
Hold the store unlocked and serve it over a socket.
Options:
.BI \-\-store " PATH,"
.BI \-\-socket " PATH,"
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " P"
and
.BI \-\-allow " EXE"
(repeatable; an exact-path allow-list).
.TP
.B get " NAME [--socket P]"
Ask a running agent for one secret.
.TP
.B probe " [--json]"
What this host allows, and what that means;\~\-\-json is machine-readable.
.TP
.B doctor " [--json] [--require LIST]"
The store, the agent and the verdict, in one report.
LIST names the guarantees the caller depends on: redact, exact,
isolate, secret\-mem, mlock, no\-new\-privs, non\-dumpable, landlock.
Exit status is 0 when every requested guarantee is active, 3 when one
is degraded and 4 when one is unavailable.
.TP
.BR help ", " version
Print the help text or the version.
.SH ALIASES
.BR \-a " (= \-\-label), " \-a\-
(= \-l\-),
.BR \-E " (= \-\-keep\-env), " \-F " (= \-\-no\-follow), " \-K " (= \-\-no\-exit\-kill)."
.B \-f
and
.B \-c
are accepted and have no effect.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PMASK_NO_SELFMASK
Do not redact the supervisor's own /proc entry.
Use only for debugging: the supervisor then shows its real command line,
which contains the secrets it was given.
.SH LABEL LIMITS
The label is cut in three places.
.B comm
keeps 15 bytes.
The exec entry scratch keeps 254 bytes of the label until the exec
completes.
The in-place redaction, used when the kernel window is not moved, cuts
the label to the span of the argument area the kernel copied.
.PP
By default the completion path builds a block with the full label and
moves the kernel's argv window onto it, so a settled process shows the
whole label.
.B \-\-no\-exact
and a kernel that refuses
.BR PR_SET_MM_MAP
leave the window where it was, and the label stays cut to that span.
A warning is printed once, unless
.BR \-\-quiet ,
when the label is longer than 254 bytes.
.SH SESSION SUMMARY
When any exec was degraded, the session prints one line:
.PP
.RS
pmask: \fIN\fR of \fIM\fR execs degraded (\fIreasons\fR)
.RE
.PP
Every entry is a degradation, not a disclosure: the exec was redacted,
or one part of the treatment was skipped.
.B unredacted
is the entry where the real strings may have been visible.
The reasons are
.BR script ,
.BR reexec ,
.BR unredacted ,
.BR comm\-not\-set
and
.BR foreign\-arch ,
with a count when a reason occurred more than once.
.PP
Three further lines, each prefixed with
.BR pmask: ,
appear when they apply:
.BI "N of M execs kept the kernel's argv and environ windows" " (PR_SET_MM_MAP refused),"
.BI "N tracee(s) left detached after their exec" " (--detach: their later execs are not redacted),"
and
.B "N secret descriptor(s) could not be closed in a descendant."
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses all of them.
.SH DAEMON MODE
With
.BI \-\-daemon " PATH"
the job runs inside a
.BR pmaskd (8).
The daemon owns the environment and the masking policy.
Mask options other than
.B \-\-label
and
.B \-\-label\-empty
have no effect there and produce a warning.
.BR \-\-secret ,
.BR \-\-isolate ,
.B \-\-no\-redact
and
.B \-\-image
are refused with exit status 255, because the daemon cannot honour
them.
.B \-\-report
is refused with exit status 255; the daemon returns the exit status only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The contract has three kinds of status.
.TP
.B payload
The exit status of the supervised command passes through untouched,
0 to 255 as the program returned it, including through
.BR \-\-daemon .
A command killed by a signal makes
.B pmask
die by the same signal.
.TP
.B "125"
The child could not install a delivered secret descriptor.
.TP
.B "126"
The
.B \-\-rexec
image was refused, or the
.B \-\-watch\-rename
filter could not be installed.
.TP
.B "127"
The image was missing, or the program could not be executed.
.TP
.B "1"
A named secret does not exist:
.BR "store get" ,
.BR "store rm" ,
.B "pmask read"
and
.B pmask get
use it so a script can tell "no such secret" from a usage error.
An unreadable descriptor in
.B pmask read
also exits 1.
.TP
.B "2"
.B pmask read
refused to write a secret to a terminal without
.BR \-\-tty .
.TP
.B "3, 4"
.B pmask doctor
found a requested guarantee degraded (3) or unavailable (4).
.TP
.B "255"
Usage errors, refusals and a wrong or missing passphrase.
This includes an unknown option, a bad label or
.BR comm ,
a refused
.BR --report ,
a refused
.BR --audit ,
a refused flag in
.B \-\-daemon
mode, an unreadable store, and a
.B doctor
request that names no known guarantee.
.PP
Store and agent errors that are not a missing name use 255; a missing
name uses 1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pmaskd (8)
